<!-- =============================== case 03 ================================ -->
<section class="section">
    <div class="shell split wide-left reveal">
        <div>
            <span class="eyebrow">Capital Equipment</span>
            <h2>A textile machine turned back at the border</h2>
            <p>
                <strong>The challenge.</strong> A machinery builder had a consignment
                held at the port of entry because the timber case carried no valid
                ISPM-15 mark — their previous supplier had sub-contracted the heat
                treatment and the certificate did not match the stamp. The machine sat
                for eleven days while it was resolved, and the installation crew was
                already on site.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>What we supplied.</strong> We build and treat in our own
                registered chamber, so the stamp and the certificate come from the same
                place. For this programme we designed a sea-worthy case with a
                bolt-down steel cradle, VCI-lined internal wrap for the exposed
                spindles, and lifting points positioned from the machine's actual centre
                of gravity rather than the geometric centre.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>The outcome.</strong> Twenty-two machines have shipped on this
                specification with no border hold and no transit damage. The case
                drawings are now issued with the machine's own documentation set.
            </p>

            <ul class="check-list">
                <li><a asp-controller="Products" asp-action="Detail" asp-route-slug="ispm-15-pallets-crates">ISPM-15 Pallets &amp; Crates</a> — sea-worthy case with cradle</li>
                <li><a asp-controller="Products" asp-action="Detail" asp-route-slug="vci-anti-corrosion-film">VCI Anti-Corrosion Film</a> — 150 micron internal liner</li>
                <li><a asp-controller="Products" asp-action="Detail" asp-route-slug="lashing-cargo-securing">Lashing &amp; Cargo Securing</a> — one-way composite, 2,500 daN</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
            <div class="media-frame" style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 2rem;text-align:center;color:#fff">
                <b style="display:block;font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:4.4rem;line-height:1;color:var(--orange)">22</b>
                <span style="display:block;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;font-size:.8rem;margin-top:.6rem;opacity:.85">
                    Machines shipped
                </span>
                <p style="margin:1.4rem 0 0;font-size:.93rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,.7)">
                    No border holds, no transit damage, since moving the case build and
                    the heat treatment under one roof.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- =============================== case 04 ================================ -->
<section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell split wide-right reveal">
        <div>
            <div class="media-frame" style="background:var(--white);padding:2.6rem 2rem;border:1px solid var(--border)">
                <span class="eyebrow">At a glance</span>
                <table class="spec-table">
                    <tr><th>Lane</th><td>Tuticorin &rarr; Felixstowe</td></tr>
                    <tr><th>Transit</th><td>24 days</td></tr>
                    <tr><th>Equipment</th><td>40 ft, FCL</td></tr>
                    <tr><th>Cargo</th><td>Baled garments on pallets</td></tr>
                    <tr><th>Change</th><td>Blown &rarr; pre-stretched film</td></tr>
                    <tr><th>Film saved</th><td>34% by weight</td></tr>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div>
            <span class="eyebrow">Textiles &amp; Apparel</span>
            <h2>Cutting film weight without losing the load</h2>
            <p>
                <strong>The challenge.</strong> A garment exporter was wrapping pallets
                with heavy blown film applied by hand, using far more material than the
                load needed while still getting stack lean in transit. Their buyer had
                also started asking for packaging weight figures as part of a
                sustainability report, and nobody could produce them.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>What we supplied.</strong> We moved them to a 17-micron
                pre-stretched film with a defined wrap pattern — two base wraps, a
                spiral up, two top wraps — and supplied edge boards on the four
                verticals so the strap could be tensioned properly. We also gave them
                the per-pallet film weight so the reporting question answered itself.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>The outcome.</strong> Film consumption dropped 34% by weight per
                pallet. Stack lean on arrival stopped being reported. The wrap pattern
                is now written into their packing instruction rather than left to
                whoever is on the machine.
            </p>

            <ul class="check-list">
                <li><a asp-controller="Products" asp-action="Detail" asp-route-slug="stretch-shrink-film">Stretch &amp; Shrink Film</a> — 17 micron pre-stretched</li>
                <li><a asp-controller="Products" asp-action="Detail" asp-route-slug="edge-corner-protection">Edge &amp; Corner Protection</a> — 35 mm recycled board angle</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- =============================== case 05 ================================ -->
<section class="section">
    <div class="shell split wide-left reveal">
        <div>
            <span class="eyebrow">Project Cargo</span>
            <h2>Securing an out-of-gauge load on a flat rack</h2>
            <p>
                <strong>The challenge.</strong> A fabricator needed to move a 14-tonne
                pressure vessel on a 40 ft flat rack from Hazira to Rotterdam. Their
                freight forwarder wanted a securing plan referenced to the CTU Code
                before the booking would be confirmed, and the fabricator had no one in
                house who could produce one.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>What we supplied.</strong> We calculated the restraint forces
                for the declared sea area, specified 32 mm one-way composite lashing at
                8,000 daN with matched ratchet tensioners, and supplied timber cradles
                cut to the vessel's contour. The plan was issued as a marked drawing
                showing every lashing angle and anchor point.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>The outcome.</strong> The booking was confirmed on first
                submission. The vessel arrived with no movement recorded against the
                cradle witness marks, and the same plan has since been reused for four
                further units of the same design.
            </p>

            <ul class="check-list">
                <li><a asp-controller="Products" asp-action="Detail" asp-route-slug="lashing-cargo-securing">Lashing &amp; Cargo Securing</a> — 32 mm composite, 8,000 daN</li>
                <li><a asp-controller="Products" asp-action="Detail" asp-route-slug="ispm-15-pallets-crates">ISPM-15 Pallets &amp; Crates</a> — contour-cut timber cradles</li>
                <li><a asp-controller="Products" asp-action="Detail" asp-route-slug="steel-strapping-seals">Steel Strapping &amp; Seals</a> — 25 mm, cradle banding</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
            <div class="media-frame" style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 2rem;text-align:center;color:#fff">
                <b style="display:block;font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:4.4rem;line-height:1;color:var(--orange)">14t</b>
                <span style="display:block;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;font-size:.8rem;margin-top:.6rem;opacity:.85">
                    Out-of-gauge unit
                </span>
                <p style="margin:1.4rem 0 0;font-size:.93rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,.7)">
                    Securing plan accepted by the forwarder on first submission, and
                    reused on four further units since.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- =============================== case 06 ================================ -->
<section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell split wide-right reveal">
        <div>
            <div class="media-frame" style="background:var(--white);padding:2.6rem 2rem;border:1px solid var(--border)">
                <span class="eyebrow">At a glance</span>
                <table class="spec-table">
                    <tr><th>Lane</th><td>Nhava Sheva &rarr; Santos</td></tr>
                    <tr><th>Transit</th><td>31 days</td></tr>
                    <tr><th>Equipment</th><td>20 ft, FCL</td></tr>
                    <tr><th>Cargo</th><td>Drummed intermediates</td></tr>
                    <tr><th>Issue</th><td>Drum-to-drum abrasion</td></tr>
                    <tr><th>Result</th><td>Zero leaks in 96 containers</td></tr>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div>
            <span class="eyebrow">Chemical &amp; Pharmaceutical</span>
            <h2>Stopping drums from working loose in transit</h2>
            <p>
                <strong>The challenge.</strong> A chemical intermediates producer was
                shipping 200-litre drums to Brazil on a 31-day transit. Drums were
                arriving scuffed through to bare metal where they had rubbed against
                each other, and on two occasions a seam had started to weep — a
                containment problem, not just a cosmetic one.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>What we supplied.</strong> Level 4 woven polypropylene dunnage
                bags between every drum row, sized to the actual 180 mm gap rather than
                stuffed in, plus steel strapping over corner protection to hold the
                palletised rows as single blocks. We had their packers measure the gaps
                on three loads before we specified anything.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>The outcome.</strong> No leak and no significant abrasion across
                the 96 containers shipped since. The measurement step is now part of
                their loading checklist.
            </p>

            <ul class="check-list">
                <li><a asp-controller="Products" asp-action="Detail" asp-route-slug="dunnage-air-bags">Dunnage Air Bags</a> — Level 4 woven PP</li>
                <li><a asp-controller="Products" asp-action="Detail" asp-route-slug="steel-strapping-seals">Steel Strapping &amp; Seals</a> — 19 mm, 1,800 kgf</li>
                <li><a asp-controller="Products" asp-action="Detail" asp-route-slug="edge-corner-protection">Edge &amp; Corner Protection</a> — PP angle, wet environment</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

